1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,636
Aarav cuts open an old charger cable.

2
00:00:02,986 --> 00:00:04,703
Inside is shiny copper.

3
00:00:04,703 --> 00:00:06,639
Around it is soft plastic.

4
00:00:06,989 --> 00:00:08,536
Both are full of electrons.

5
00:00:08,536 --> 00:00:10,841
So why does only the copper carry current?

6
00:00:11,341 --> 00:00:13,982
And the chip in his
phone is made of silicon.

7
00:00:14,332 --> 00:00:16,689
Silicon carries current only sometimes.

8
00:00:16,689 --> 00:00:18,000
Let's find out why.

9
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:20,738
Picture a building with two floors.

10
00:00:21,088 --> 00:00:23,576
The ground floor is packed with people.

11
00:00:23,576 --> 00:00:24,744
Nobody can move.

12
00:00:25,094 --> 00:00:26,862
The upper floor is empty.

13
00:00:26,862 --> 00:00:29,131
Up there, anyone can run freely.

14
00:00:29,481 --> 00:00:31,232
Between the floors is a jump.

15
00:00:31,232 --> 00:00:32,922
Its height decides everything.

16
00:00:33,572 --> 00:00:36,432
Electrons in a solid live in
a building just like this.

17
00:00:36,782 --> 00:00:41,351
In a single atom, an electron can
sit only on fixed energy steps.

18
00:00:41,951 --> 00:00:46,629
Pack billions of atoms into a crystal,
and each step spreads into a band.

19
00:00:47,229 --> 00:00:51,489
A band is a crowd of energy
levels, packed very close together.

20
00:00:52,089 --> 00:00:54,324
The lower band is the valence band.

21
00:00:54,324 --> 00:00:57,224
It holds the outer
electrons, and it is full.

22
00:00:57,824 --> 00:01:00,051
The upper band is the conduction band.

23
00:01:00,051 --> 00:01:02,192
Electrons up there are free to move.

24
00:01:02,792 --> 00:01:05,955
Between them is the
forbidden energy gap, E g.

25
00:01:05,955 --> 00:01:08,097
No electron can stay inside it.

26
00:01:08,697 --> 00:01:12,778
We measure the gap in electron
volts, a tiny energy unit.

27
00:01:13,128 --> 00:01:18,368
One electron volt is one point six
times ten to the minus nineteen joules.

28
00:01:19,018 --> 00:01:20,397
Now, three kinds of solids.

29
00:01:20,747 --> 00:01:24,566
In a conductor, like copper,
the two bands overlap.

30
00:01:24,916 --> 00:01:26,366
There is no gap at all.

31
00:01:26,366 --> 00:01:28,188
Electrons just walk up and move.

32
00:01:28,788 --> 00:01:32,286
In an insulator, like
diamond, the gap is huge.

33
00:01:32,636 --> 00:01:35,497
Diamond's gap is five
point four electron volts.

34
00:01:35,497 --> 00:01:37,503
Almost no electron can jump it.

35
00:01:38,103 --> 00:01:41,913
Any gap bigger than about three
electron volts makes an insulator.

36
00:01:42,513 --> 00:01:47,145
In a semiconductor, the gap is small,
less than three electron volts.

37
00:01:47,495 --> 00:01:49,151
Silicon's gap is one point one.

38
00:01:49,151 --> 00:01:51,082
Germanium's is about zero point seven.

39
00:01:51,732 --> 00:01:55,746
That is why resistivity is tiny in
metals, middling in semiconductors,

40
00:01:55,746 --> 00:01:57,421
and enormous in insulators.

41
00:01:58,071 --> 00:02:00,213
The size of the gap decides who conducts.

42
00:02:01,013 --> 00:02:02,959
Let's test this on the breadboard.

43
00:02:03,309 --> 00:02:05,833
A battery pushes current through a sample.

44
00:02:05,833 --> 00:02:07,291
A heater warms it up.

45
00:02:07,641 --> 00:02:09,083
First, a copper wire.

46
00:02:09,083 --> 00:02:10,981
Watch the current on the scope.

47
00:02:11,331 --> 00:02:14,211
As the copper warms, its
current drops a little.

48
00:02:14,211 --> 00:02:15,646
Hot metal resists more.

49
00:02:16,296 --> 00:02:18,631
Now swap in a piece of pure silicon.

50
00:02:18,981 --> 00:02:19,881
Cold silicon.

51
00:02:19,857 --> 00:02:23,438
Very few electrons have made the
jump, so the current is tiny.

52
00:02:23,788 --> 00:02:24,913
Turn up the heat.

53
00:02:24,913 --> 00:02:27,113
Electrons gain energy and jump the gap.

54
00:02:27,463 --> 00:02:31,442
Each jump leaves an empty spot,
a hole, in the valence band.

55
00:02:31,792 --> 00:02:33,719
More jumpers, more current.

56
00:02:33,719 --> 00:02:35,347
The scope trace climbs.

57
00:02:35,997 --> 00:02:38,698
So a semiconductor's
resistance falls as it warms.

58
00:02:38,698 --> 00:02:40,625
That is the opposite of a metal.

59
00:02:41,275 --> 00:02:45,347
At absolute zero, the coldest
possible temperature, nobody jumps.

60
00:02:45,347 --> 00:02:47,805
Pure silicon behaves like an insulator.

61
00:02:48,455 --> 00:02:49,355
Worked example.

62
00:02:49,313 --> 00:02:51,317
Light can also give electrons the push.

63
00:02:51,667 --> 00:02:55,418
What is the longest wavelength of
light that can lift an electron across

64
00:02:55,418 --> 00:02:56,318
silicon's gap?

65
00:02:56,632 --> 00:02:58,560
First, the gap in joules.

66
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:02,954
One point one times one point six
times ten to the minus nineteen.

67
00:03:03,654 --> 00:03:08,076
That is one point seven six times
ten to the minus nineteen joules.

68
00:03:08,776 --> 00:03:11,668
The energy of light is h c over lambda.

69
00:03:11,668 --> 00:03:14,770
So lambda equals h c
divided by the energy.

70
00:03:15,470 --> 00:03:20,126
h c is about one point nine nine
times ten to the minus twenty five.

71
00:03:20,826 --> 00:03:24,626
Lambda comes out near one thousand
one hundred and thirty nanometres.

72
00:03:24,626 --> 00:03:26,056
That is infrared light.

73
00:03:26,706 --> 00:03:30,857
A handy shortcut: lambda in nanometres
is about twelve forty, divided by

74
00:03:30,857 --> 00:03:32,538
the gap in electron volts.

75
00:03:33,188 --> 00:03:34,650
Now, the exam trap.

76
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,208
Many students think heating
always raises resistance.

77
00:03:38,208 --> 00:03:40,199
That is true only for metals.

78
00:03:40,549 --> 00:03:43,718
Heat a semiconductor, and
more electrons jump the gap.

79
00:03:43,718 --> 00:03:45,254
Its resistance goes down.

80
00:03:45,904 --> 00:03:47,202
Where is this asked?

81
00:03:47,202 --> 00:03:51,104
PUC, CBSE and ISC boards ask you
to draw and compare band diagrams.

82
00:03:51,454 --> 00:03:55,675
JEE Main links band gap with light,
as in September twenty twenty.

83
00:03:56,325 --> 00:03:57,225
Try this.

84
00:03:57,035 --> 00:04:01,295
Light of wavelength six hundred and twenty
nanometres falls on three materials.

85
00:04:01,295 --> 00:04:05,313
Their gaps are one point one, two
point five and five point four electron

86
00:04:05,313 --> 00:04:06,213
volts.

87
00:04:05,842 --> 00:04:07,885
Which can it push electrons across?

88
00:04:13,285 --> 00:04:14,185
A.

89
00:04:13,677 --> 00:04:16,573
Only the one point one
electron volt material.

90
00:04:16,923 --> 00:04:20,694
Photon energy is twelve
forty divided by six twenty.

91
00:04:20,694 --> 00:04:22,807
That is two electron volts.

92
00:04:23,157 --> 00:04:27,101
An electron jumps only if the
light's energy is at least the gap.

93
00:04:27,451 --> 00:04:31,980
Two is more than one point one, but
less than two point five and five point

94
00:04:31,980 --> 00:04:32,880
four.

95
00:04:33,152 --> 00:04:34,699
Screenshot this card.

96
00:04:37,899 --> 00:04:40,644
Next, intrinsic and
extrinsic semiconductors.

97
00:04:40,644 --> 00:04:44,497
How can one impurity atom in a
million change silicon so much?
